Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Long-standing hypertension, Progressive renal decline, Bland urinalysis/mild proteinuria

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-019
Reason for Visit: Severe, throbbing occipital headache, blurring of vision, and extreme lethargy, requiring urgent assessment to rule out hypertensive emergency and acute cerebrovascular event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient with a known history of chronic, poorly controlled hypertension who acutely developed a severe headache, visual changes, and profound fatigue. Urgent after-hours assessment was absolutely mandated to measure blood pressure, perform a neurological examination, and determine if the patient was experiencing a hypertensive emergency requiring immediate hospital transfer to prevent acute end-organ infarction or acute-on-chronic renal failure.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head is throbbing relentlessly, my vision keeps blurring, and I am too exhausted to even stand up."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a long-standing history of poorly controlled chronic hypertension. Over the past several years, they have experienced a gradual, progressive decline in renal function. Their presentation has been largely asymptomatic until the last 48 hours, when they developed profound lethargy and a severe, continuous occipital headache. They report frequent nocturia, representing an early loss of urinary concentrating ability. Tonight, the headache acutely worsened, accompanied by transient episodes of blurred vision. They deny chest pain, shortness of breath, or focal limb weakness.
Functional Status: Resting in bed, unable to perform daily activities due to the severe headache and lethargy.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of stroke; patient lives alone and has historically poor adherence to medical regimens.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 215/115 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, squinting due to photophobia, holding the back of their head.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Severe hypertension confirmed on multiple readings. A sustained left ventricular heave is palpable, indicating chronic end-organ damage. Neurological: Cranial nerves grossly intact, no focal motor or sensory deficits. Ophthalmic: Fundoscopic examination reveals severe hypertensive changes, including marked arteriovenous nicking and silver-wiring, but no frank papilloedema. Genitourinary: Point-of-care urinalysis demonstrates mild to moderate non-nephrotic range proteinuria with a complete absence of active urinary sediment such as red blood cell casts. Expected investigation results include persistently elevated serum creatinine and bilaterally small echogenic kidneys on ultrasound.
Environmental Observations: Antihypertensive medication bottles are mostly full, indicating significant non-compliance over the preceding months.
Medication Reconciliation: Prescribed amlodipine and perindopril; patient admits to taking them only sporadically "when feeling tense."

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis - Status: Exacerbated (Hypertensive Urgency)
Differentials:

Diabetic Nephropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of massive (nephrotic range) proteinuria and no known history of diabetes mellitus.

Acute Glomerulonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the bland urinalysis, specifically the lack of red blood cell casts or active sediment.

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely intact neurological examination without any focal motor, sensory, or speech deficits.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe hypertensive urgency, driven by long-standing non-compliance, resulting in acute, symptomatic end-organ strain (severe headache, visual blurring) superimposed on chronic hypertensive nephrosclerosis (proteinuria, nocturia, LVH). While gross focal neurological deficits are absent, the extreme blood pressure elevation places the patient at imminent risk for an acute cerebrovascular accident or irreversible acceleration of renal failure. Urgent after-hours physical and neurological assessment was strictly necessary to safely triage the condition, carefully reinstate oral antihypertensive therapy to lower the pressure gradually, and prevent a catastrophic vascular event before the next available routine clinic appointment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Amlodipine 10mg immediately under observation. Instructed the patient to remain resting in a quiet, dark room to minimize sensory input and further sympathetic activation. Ordered an urgent comprehensive metabolic panel for the morning to quantify the degree of acute-on-chronic renal impairment.
Positive Behavioral Change: To address the chronic medication non-compliance, the patient is instructed to use a weekly pill organizer placed directly next to their toothbrush. By coupling the unfamiliar habit of taking daily medication with the ingrained, automatic habit of brushing teeth, the patient creates a reliable trigger, ensuring consistent blood pressure control and preventing future hypertensive crises.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): Home blood pressure cuff recommended for daily monitoring.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review tomorrow afternoon to recheck blood pressure and review urgent renal function blood tests.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer via 000 is required if the patient develops weakness on one side of their body, slurred speech, sudden severe chest pain, or a worsening, explosive "thunderclap" headache.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The blood pressure is dangerously high; it must be brought down slowly over days to prevent a stroke from sudden pressure drops.
Clinical Course: The headache should slowly improve as the medication takes effect over the next several hours. The kidney damage is chronic but can be stabilized with strict blood pressure control.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you experience sudden weakness in your face or arm, if you cannot speak clearly, or if the headache becomes suddenly explosive and unbearable, call 000 immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Years of high blood pressure have slowly damaged the tiny blood vessels in your kidneys and eyes. Tonight, the pressure spiked dangerously high, causing your severe headache. The medication will slowly relax the blood vessels to bring the pressure down safely.
Actionable Steps: Take your blood pressure medication every single day without fail, even if you feel perfectly well. Use a pillbox next to your toothbrush to remember. Attend the urgent blood test tomorrow morning.

